    <!-- SECTION ONE: DPT_DynamicBiomeDemoController -->
    <h2 id="dynamic-biome-controller">1. DPT_DynamicBiomeDemoController</h2>
    <p>The <code>DPT_DynamicBiomeDemoController</code> is an in-editor and runtime driver component designed to modulate terrain shader properties on the fly. It exposes live controls for biome weights, wetness transitions, snow accumulation, and emissive pulse effects without requiring expensive mesh re-uploads or CPU vertex operations[cite: 6].</p>

    <h3>General & Target Setup</h3>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th style="width: 30%;">Property</th>
                <th>Description</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Terrain Material</strong></td>
                <td>The material instance to control. If left unassigned, the component automatically resolves the <code>sharedMaterial</code> from the local <code>Renderer</code> component on Awake.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Enable Dynamic Biomes</strong></td>
                <td>Master boolean toggle mapped to <code>_Enable_Dynamic_Biomes</code>. When disabled, the terrain shader bypasses all live modifier calculations with zero ALU overhead[cite: 6].</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Dynamic Slot Configuration</h3>
    <p>The component exposes four configurable modifier channels corresponding to <code>_Dynamic_Biome_Slot1</code> through <code>_Dynamic_Biome_Slot4</code> and their paired <code>_Params</code> companion vectors[cite: 6, 7]:</p>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th style="width: 30%;">Setting</th>
                <th>Description</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Active</strong></td>
                <td>Toggles whether this individual slot modifier evaluates in the shader. (Note: Slot 1 is always active when the Master Switch is enabled)[cite: 6].</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Target Biome ID</strong></td>
                <td>The index of the biome to modulate (e.g., 0 for Lush Grass, 6 for Mountain Snow, 9 for Magma Crust)[cite: 3]. Set to <code>-1</code> or toggle off to disable[cite: 6].</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Weight Multiplier</strong></td>
                <td>Scalar multiplier applied to the target biome's raw vertex weight before normalization[cite: 6]. <code>1.0</code> is neutral, <code>0.0</code> suppresses the biome entirely, and values above <code>1.0</code> force progressive expansion[cite: 6].</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Smoothness Mult</strong></td>
                <td>Multiplies glossiness and specular highlights on the target biome[cite: 6]. Ideal for simulating surface wetness, pooled water, or icy glaze[cite: 6].</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Normal Strength Mult</strong></td>
                <td>Scales micro-bump relief on the target biome[cite: 6]. Lower values flatten surface bumps to mimic standing liquid[cite: 6].</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Parallax Depth Mult</strong></td>
                <td>Scales Parallax Occlusion Mapping extrusion depth[cite: 6]. Raising this parameter makes snow, volcanic rock, or foliage physically deeper in relief[cite: 6].</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Emissive Mult</strong></td>
                <td>Direct multiplier for the biome's emissive tint and vein mask, driving glowing lava cracks or bioluminescence[cite: 6].</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Simulation Presets</h3>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th style="width: 30%;">Feature</th>
                <th>Description</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Animate Snow Accumulation</strong></td>
                <td>When enabled in Play mode, runs a sine-wave simulation that continuously transitions Slot 1 from a light dusting (0.0x weight) to a deep snow blanket (3.5x weight with increased parallax relief).</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Snow Cycle Speed</strong></td>
                <td>Controls the oscillation frequency of the snow accumulation and melting weather loop.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Animate Emissive Pulse</strong></td>
                <td>When enabled in Play mode, applies a rhythmic glow animation to Slot 4 (targeted to Magma Crust by default) to simulate volcanic activity.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Pulse Speed</strong></td>
                <td>Sets the pulse frequency of the emissive sine wave.</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="perf">
        <strong>Performance Note:</strong> This controller modifies material properties via standard <code>Material.SetVector</code> calls[cite: 6]. Because all data packs directly into the <code>UnityPerMaterial</code> constant buffer, it is 100% SRP Batcher compatible and produces zero additional draw calls or garbage collection allocations.
    </div>

    <!-- SECTION TWO: DPT_SurfaceRaycastTester -->
    <h2 id="surface-raycast-tester">2. DPT_SurfaceRaycastTester</h2>
    <p><code>DPT_SurfaceRaycastTester</code> is a multi-mode diagnostic and probing utility. It performs real-time raycasts against terrain geometry, queries the baked <code>DPT_TerrainSurfaceData</code> ledger through <code>DPT_TerrainSurfaceTarget</code>, and resolves the current dominant biome—factoring in active live weather and growth modifiers.</p>

    <h3>Raycast & Probing Configuration</h3>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th style="width: 30%;">Property</th>
                <th>Description</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Raycast Mode</strong></td>
                <td>Selects the probing origin and direction:<br>
                &bull; <code>DownwardProbe</code>: Casts straight down (-Y) from the GameObject transform (standard for player footsteps).<br>
                &bull; <code>TransformForward</code>: Casts along the forward vector (+Z) of the transform.<br>
                &bull; <code>MouseCursor</code>: Casts rays from the scene/main camera through the mouse pointer for point-and-click inspection.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Max Ray Distance</strong></td>
                <td>Maximum distance (in meters) the ray will travel before reporting a miss. Default: <code>50m</code>.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Raycast Layers</strong></td>
                <td><code>LayerMask</code> filter used to ensure rays only hit terrain colliders while ignoring triggers, props, or actors.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Override Surface Data</strong></td>
                <td>Optional field allowing you to manually assign a <code>DPT_TerrainSurfaceData</code> asset directly, bypassing the target component on the hit collider.</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>HUD & Visualization Settings</h3>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th style="width: 30%;">Setting</th>
                <th>Description</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Show Scene Gizmos</strong></td>
                <td>Draws green impact lines, cyan surface normal rays, and Scene view handle tags displaying the hit triangle and biome metadata.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Show Game HUD</strong></td>
                <td>Renders an immediate-mode on-screen GUI box in the top-left corner displaying the hit object, triangle index, resolved biome name, and world normal.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Log Clicks to Console</strong></td>
                <td>When enabled, left-clicking while aiming at a valid terrain surface prints full debug telemetry to the Unity Console.</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="tip">
        <strong>Tip for Footstep Setup:</strong> In your final game, replace this tester with a call to <code>DPT_TerrainSurfaceTarget.TryGetHitBiome(hit, out int biomeID, out string biomeName)</code> inside your player movement or animation event scripts. It executes in ~10 nanoseconds with zero GC allocation.
    </div>

